\section{Grammar Composition}\label{sec:grammar-composition-lw}

\subsection{The Need for Composable Grammars}

Real-world languages are rarely monolithic. They are composed of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A core language (e.g., expressions, statements).
  \item Extension constructs (e.g., pattern matching, concurrency primitives).
  \item Embedded DSLs (e.g., SQL queries in a host language).
  \item Annotations and pragmas.
\end{itemize}

Traditional grammar formalisms do not support this kind of modular composition. Grammar composition addresses this need.

\subsection{Grammar Composition Operators}

\begin{definition}[Grammar Composition Operators]\label{def:grammar-ops}
The main grammar composition operators are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Union} ($G_1 \cup G_2$): Combine the productions of two grammars.
  \item \textbf{Override} ($G_1 \triangleright G_2$): $G_2$'s productions override $G_1$'s for nonterminals defined in both.
  \item \textbf{Restriction} ($G_1 \setminus G_2$): Remove $G_2$'s productions from $G_1$.
  \item \textbf{Parameterization} ($G[P]$): A grammar parameterized by another grammar $P$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Challenges of Grammar Composition}

Grammar composition introduces several challenges:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Ambiguity}: Composing two unambiguous grammars may produce an ambiguous grammar. This requires generalized parsing (GLR/G LL) or disambiguation filters.
  \item \textbf{Conflicts}: Different grammars may define conflicting rules for the same nonterminal.
  \item \textbf{Semantics}: The semantics of the composed language must be defined, which may require composing semantic analyses.
  \item \textbf{Tooling}: Editor services (completion, highlighting) must be generated for the composed language.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Projectional Editing}\label{sec:projectional-editing}

\subsection{The Concept}

Projectional editing inverts the traditional relationship between text and AST:

